// Unit 1 "Money Moves", Chapter 4: How the Market Works
export default {
  id: 'money-ch4',
  title: 'How the Market Works',
  blurb: 'Exchanges, market hours, order types, and the hidden plumbing that moves your trade from a tap on your phone to shares in your account.',
  lessons: [
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Lesson 1: What the stock market is
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    {
      id: 'money-4-what-is-the-stock-market',
      title: 'What Is "the Stock Market," Really?',
      summary: 'Exchanges, dark pools, brokers, and the question nobody asks: who is on the other side of your trade?',
      minutes: 13,
      icon: 'globe',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Not one place, a network', html: `<p>On the news, "the stock market" sounds like one giant building where prices are decided. In reality it is a <strong>network</strong>: a bunch of exchanges, other trading venues, brokers, and very fast computers, all connected and all competing to match buyers with sellers.</p><p>There are two different markets hiding inside that phrase:</p><ul><li>The <span class="term" data-def="Where a company sells brand-new shares to investors and receives the money, like in an IPO.">primary market</span>: a company sells new shares, such as in an IPO, and the company gets the cash.</li><li>The <span class="term" data-def="Where investors trade existing shares with each other. The company does not get the money.">secondary market</span>: investors trade those shares with each other afterward.</li></ul><p>Almost every trade you will ever make happens in the secondary market. When Maya buys one share of a sneaker company, her money goes to another investor who is selling, not to the company.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: "Fun fact: the famous trading floor in New York still exists, but the vast majority of trades are matched by computers in data centers." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The exchanges', html: `<p>An <span class="term" data-def="A regulated marketplace where buy and sell orders for securities are matched.">exchange</span> is a regulated marketplace that matches buy and sell orders. The big US names:</p><ul><li><strong>NYSE</strong> (New York Stock Exchange): traces its roots to 1792 and still has the famous floor on Wall Street.</li><li><strong>Nasdaq</strong>: launched in 1971 as the first electronic stock market, home to many tech companies.</li><li><strong>Cboe</strong>: started as an options exchange in Chicago and now runs several stock exchanges too.</li></ul><p>Those three companies own most of the exchanges, but there are newer ones as well, such as IEX, MEMX, and MIAX. In total, the US has more than a dozen registered stock exchanges.</p><p>A key detail: a stock is <em>listed</em> on one exchange, but it can <em>trade</em> on nearly all of them. A Nasdaq-listed stock trades on NYSE venues, Cboe venues, and more. They all compete for your order.</p>` },
        { type: 'match', prompt: 'Match each venue to its description.', pairs: [ { left: 'NYSE', right: 'Exchange with roots in 1792 and a famous trading floor' }, { left: 'Nasdaq', right: 'First electronic stock market, launched in 1971' }, { left: 'Dark pool', right: 'Trading venue that does not display orders publicly before they trade' }, { left: 'Broker', right: 'Firm that sends your orders to the market for you' } ] },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Off-exchange trading and dark pools', html: `<p>Not every trade happens on an exchange. A large share of US stock volume, often close to half in recent years, happens <strong>off-exchange</strong>.</p><ul><li><span class="term" data-def="Alternative trading system: a SEC-regulated venue that matches trades but is not a registered exchange.">Alternative trading systems (ATSs)</span> are regulated venues that match trades without being full exchanges.</li><li><strong>Dark pools</strong> are ATSs that do not show their orders publicly before trading. Big institutions use them to move huge blocks of shares without tipping off the market. If a pension fund announced "selling 2 million shares," prices would drop before it finished.</li><li><strong>Wholesalers</strong> are big market-making firms that fill many retail investors' orders directly, out of their own inventory.</li></ul><p>"Dark" sounds sinister, but these trades are still regulated and are reported publicly right after they happen. The darkness is about the order before it trades, not the trade itself.</p>` },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'A stock listed on Nasdaq can only be bought and sold on Nasdaq.', answer: false, explain: 'Listing is where a company signs up. Trading happens across many exchanges and off-exchange venues that all compete to fill orders in that stock.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Brokers: your door into the market', html: `<p>You cannot walk up to the NYSE and buy a share. You go through a <span class="term" data-def="A firm licensed to buy and sell securities on behalf of customers.">broker</span>, the company behind your investing app or brokerage account.</p><p>When you tap Buy, the broker takes your order, decides where to send it, makes sure it is filled, and records the shares in your account. Your shares are usually held in "street name," meaning the broker's custodian holds them on your behalf, while you remain the owner.</p><p>Not long ago, a trade could cost $7 to $10 in commissions. Around 2019, most major US brokers cut stock and ETF commissions to $0. That is great for small investors, but "free" trading has to be paid for somehow. We will look at how in the life-of-a-trade lesson.</p><p>You generally need to be 18 to open a brokerage account in your own name. Teens usually start with a custodial account an adult controls.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Who is on the other side?', html: `<p>Every trade has two sides. For every share bought, someone sold it. So when Dev buys because he is sure a stock is about to rocket, ask a simple question: <em>who is selling to him, and why?</em></p><p>The other side could be:</p><ul><li>A <strong>market maker</strong>, a firm that buys and sells all day to profit from small price gaps.</li><li>A pension fund or mutual fund rebalancing billions of dollars.</li><li>A hedge fund with a team of analysts and faster data than Dev.</li><li>An algorithm that reacts in microseconds.</li><li>Another regular person who needs cash for rent.</li></ul><p>Sometimes the seller is not betting against you at all. They just need money or are adjusting a portfolio. But when you are trading short-term on a hunch, you are often competing against professionals with better tools. That is a big reason short-term trading is so hard to win at.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "If you are sure a stock is a steal, remember someone just sold it to you. Ask what they might know that you do not." } },
        { type: 'check', question: 'Maya buys 2 shares of a company that went public five years ago. Where does her money go?', options: ['To the company, to fund new projects', 'To the investor or firm that sold her the shares', 'To the exchange, which keeps it', 'To the SEC'], answer: 1, explain: 'This is the secondary market. The company only received money when it originally sold shares. Now investors trade existing shares with each other.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Why have a market at all?', html: `<p>Stock markets do three useful jobs.</p><ol><li><strong>Raising money.</strong> Companies sell shares to fund factories, research, and hiring. Investors get a slice of the future in return.</li><li><strong>Liquidity.</strong> Because there is always a market, you can turn shares back into cash in seconds during trading hours. Without that, owning part of a business would be like owning part of a restaurant: hard to sell quickly.</li><li><strong>Price discovery.</strong> Millions of buyers and sellers constantly vote with their money. The price reflects what all of them, together, think a company is worth right now.</li></ol><p>Ms. Ortiz cannot easily sell 1% of her bakery tomorrow. A shareholder of a public company can sell 1% of their shares before lunch. That ease of buying and selling is a big reason people are willing to invest in the first place.</p><p>Healthy markets help everyone: companies get funding to grow, workers get jobs, and savers get a way to share in that growth.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'happy', say: "Markets turn 'I own a piece of a business' into something you can buy or sell in seconds. That is a big deal." } },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'ipo-path', caption: 'A private company goes public: it files with regulators, sells new shares in an IPO, and then those shares trade between investors on the secondary market.' },
        { type: 'order', prompt: 'Put the life of a newly public company\'s shares in order.', items: ['The company decides to go public', 'It files paperwork with the SEC', 'IPO shares are priced and sold to investors', 'Shares start trading on an exchange', 'Investors trade the shares with each other'], explain: 'The company only gets cash at the IPO step. Every trade after that is investors swapping existing shares.' },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Market vocabulary', cards: [ { front: 'Exchange', back: 'A regulated marketplace that matches buy and sell orders.' }, { front: 'Primary market', back: 'Where companies sell new shares and receive the money.' }, { front: 'Secondary market', back: 'Where investors trade existing shares with each other.' }, { front: 'Dark pool', back: 'A trading venue that does not display orders before they trade.' }, { front: 'Liquidity', back: 'How easily you can buy or sell without moving the price much.' } ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'Which statement about the US stock market is accurate?', options: ['It is one building in New York', 'It is a network of exchanges, off-exchange venues, and brokers', 'It only operates on the NYSE floor', 'It is run by the Federal Reserve'], answer: 1, explain: 'Trading is spread across more than a dozen exchanges plus off-exchange venues, all connected electronically.' },
          { q: 'What makes a dark pool "dark"?', options: ['It operates at night', 'It is illegal', 'Orders are not displayed publicly before they trade', 'Trades are never reported'], answer: 2, explain: 'Orders are hidden until they execute, but trades are still regulated and reported afterward.' },
          { q: 'In an IPO, who receives the money from the new shares sold?', options: ['The company issuing the shares', 'Other shareholders', 'The exchange', 'Your broker'], answer: 0, explain: 'That is the primary market. After the IPO, trades happen between investors.' },
          { q: 'Why do big institutions often use dark pools?', options: ['To avoid all regulation', 'To get free trades', 'To trade only with retail investors', 'To trade large blocks without moving the price against themselves'], answer: 3, explain: 'Showing a huge order publicly would push the price away before it filled.' },
          { q: 'Dev buys a stock on a hot tip. Who might be on the other side?', options: ['Nobody; the exchange creates new shares', 'A market maker, a fund, an algorithm, or another investor', 'Only the company itself', 'Only the SEC'], answer: 1, explain: 'Every trade has a seller. It might be a professional with better information or someone who simply needs cash.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Lesson 2: Market hours, the full picture
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    {
      id: 'money-4-market-hours',
      title: 'Market Hours: When Wall Street Is Awake',
      summary: 'The regular session, pre-market and after-hours, holidays and early closes, overnight trading, and what never sleeps.',
      minutes: 16,
      icon: 'clock',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'The main event: 9:30 to 4:00', html: `<p>The US stock market's <span class="term" data-def="The main trading hours for US stocks: 9:30am to 4:00pm Eastern Time, Monday to Friday.">regular session</span> runs <strong>9:30am to 4:00pm Eastern Time, Monday through Friday</strong>. That is 6.5 hours, and it is when the vast majority of trading happens.</p><p>Everything is quoted in Eastern Time, wherever you live. Maya is in California, so for her the opening bell rings at 6:30am and the closing bell at 1:00pm, before her afternoon shift at the café.</p><p>The first and last minutes are special. Exchanges run an <strong>opening auction</strong> that gathers overnight orders and sets one opening price, and a <strong>closing auction</strong> that sets the official closing price. A big chunk of each day's volume trades right at the close, because index funds and other big investors want that official price.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'happy', say: "9:30am ET: ding ding ding. The opening bell is my favorite sound. Well, that and dividends landing." } },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'market-sessions', caption: 'One trading day in Eastern Time: pre-market from 4:00 to 9:30am, the regular session from 9:30am to 4:00pm, and after-hours from 4:00 to 8:00pm. Notice how much thinner trading is outside the middle block.' },
        { type: 'widget', name: 'market-clock', props: {}, caption: 'This clock shows which US stock session is open right now, in Eastern Time. Check it at different times of day to see how the sessions change.' },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'How many hours long is the regular US stock market session?', answer: 6.5, tolerance: 0, unit: '', explain: '9:30am to 4:00pm Eastern is 6 hours and 30 minutes, or 6.5 hours.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Pre-market and after-hours', html: `<p>Trading does not fully stop outside 9:30 to 4:00. There are <span class="term" data-def="Trading sessions before the regular open (pre-market) and after the close (after-hours).">extended-hours</span> sessions:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Session</th><th>Typical hours (ET)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pre-market</td><td>As early as 4:00am to 9:30am</td></tr><tr><td>Regular</td><td>9:30am to 4:00pm</td></tr><tr><td>After-hours</td><td>4:00pm to 8:00pm</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The exact window depends on your broker. Many offer pre-market starting at 4:00am, while others start at 7:00am or 8:00am. Some limit after-hours to a shorter window. You may also have to opt in or accept an extra disclosure before your first extended-hours trade.</p><p>Extended hours exist mainly so people can react to news that breaks outside the regular session, like earnings reports or big world events.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Why extended hours are a different game', html: `<p>Extended hours look like the same market with the lights dimmed. They are riskier than they look.</p><ul><li><strong>Thinner liquidity.</strong> Far fewer people are trading, so there may be few shares available at any given price.</li><li><strong>Wider spreads.</strong> The gap between the best buy price and best sell price is often much bigger, so you pay more just to get in or out.</li><li><strong>Bigger, jumpier moves.</strong> A modest order can push the price around.</li><li><strong>Prices may not stick.</strong> A stock up 8% at 7:00am can open flat at 9:30am once everyone shows up.</li><li><strong>Pros everywhere.</strong> Much of the activity comes from professional traders reacting instantly to news.</li></ul><p>Because of all this, most brokers <span class="hl">require limit orders</span> in extended hours. A limit order sets the worst price you will accept, so a thin market cannot fill you at a crazy price. Many brokers also do not trigger stop orders outside the regular session.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "After-hours prices can be a mirage. Fewer traders, wider spreads, and a price that can vanish by 9:30. Always use a limit order." } },
        { type: 'check', question: 'Why do most brokers require limit orders during pre-market and after-hours trading?', options: ['Market orders are illegal after 4pm', 'Thin trading and wide spreads could fill a market order at a terrible price', 'Limit orders are cheaper to process', 'Exchanges are closed, so only brokers can trade'], answer: 1, explain: 'With few participants, the nearest available price can be far from the last trade. A limit order caps the price you are willing to pay or accept.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Earnings: before the bell or after the close', html: `<p>Public companies report results every quarter, and most release them <strong>outside regular hours</strong>: either before the open (often around 7:00 to 8:30am ET) or right after the close (often a few minutes after 4:00pm ET). That gives investors time to read the numbers before the full market reacts.</p><p>That is why you see headlines like "Stock jumps 12% after hours." Here is what really happens:</p><ol><li>The company reports at 4:05pm.</li><li>Extended-hours traders react instantly, on thin volume.</li><li>The company holds a call with analysts, often around 4:30 or 5:00pm, and the price can swing again.</li><li>The next morning, the stock may <span class="term" data-def="When a stock opens well above or below its previous close, leaving an empty space on the chart.">gap</span> up or down at the open, sometimes very differently from the after-hours price.</li></ol><p>If you own a stock, check its earnings date. That is when the biggest surprise moves usually happen.</p>` },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'If a stock is up 10% in after-hours trading, it will open 10% higher the next morning.', answer: false, explain: 'After-hours prices are set by a small number of traders. When the full market arrives at 9:30am, the opening price can be higher, lower, or roughly flat compared to the after-hours move.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Market holidays', html: `<p>The NYSE and Nasdaq close for these holidays:</p><ul><li>New Year's Day</li><li>Martin Luther King Jr. Day</li><li>Presidents Day (Washington's Birthday)</li><li>Good Friday</li><li>Memorial Day</li><li>Juneteenth</li><li>Independence Day</li><li>Labor Day</li><li>Thanksgiving Day</li><li>Christmas Day</li></ul><p>Notice the quirks. <strong>Good Friday</strong> is not a federal holiday, yet stocks close. <strong>Columbus Day</strong> and <strong>Veterans Day</strong> are federal holidays, yet stocks trade (the bond market closes, though). Juneteenth joined the list starting in 2022.</p><p>When a holiday lands on a weekend, the market usually closes the Friday before or the Monday after. And there are rare unscheduled closures, like the national day of mourning for former President Jimmy Carter in January 2025, Hurricane Sandy in 2012, and four straight trading days starting on September 11, 2001.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'wow', say: "Good Friday is not a federal holiday, but the stock market closes anyway. Columbus Day is a federal holiday, and stocks trade. Markets are quirky." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Early closes at 1:00pm', html: `<p>On a few days a year the stock market closes early, at <strong>1:00pm ET</strong>, instead of 4:00pm. The usual ones:</p><ul><li>The <strong>day after Thanksgiving</strong> (Black Friday).</li><li><strong>Christmas Eve</strong>, when it falls on a weekday.</li><li><strong>July 3</strong> in some years, depending on which day of the week July 4 lands on.</li></ul><p>Bond markets often close early on those days as well, and on some others.</p><p>Early-close days are usually quiet, with lighter volume. After-hours sessions are shortened too, and some brokers adjust their own extended-hours windows.</p><p>The schedule shifts every year based on the calendar, so never assume. The NYSE and Nasdaq post their official holiday and early-close calendars several years in advance. Check them before planning a trade around a holiday, especially if you have an order you need filled before a long weekend.</p>` },
        { type: 'match', prompt: 'Match each day to how the US stock market usually handles it.', pairs: [ { left: 'Good Friday', right: 'Closed all day' }, { left: 'Day after Thanksgiving', right: 'Early close at 1:00pm ET' }, { left: 'Columbus Day', right: 'Open for normal hours' }, { left: 'Juneteenth', right: 'Closed all day' } ] },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Overnight and the push toward 24 hours', html: `<p>This is where the clock is changing fast. Some brokers now offer <strong>overnight trading</strong> in a selection of popular stocks and ETFs, roughly from 8:00pm to 4:00am ET, Sunday night through Thursday night. Paired with pre-market, regular, and after-hours sessions, that adds up to trading around the clock, five days a week, often called "24/5."</p><p>Overnight trades have mostly run through alternative trading systems rather than the main exchanges. But the exchanges have been moving too: major exchange operators have announced plans and sought regulatory approval to extend trading toward nearly 24 hours on weekdays, and a new exchange was approved specifically to trade around the clock. The industry plumbing, like the systems that publish official prices and clear trades, has had to adapt as well.</p><p>Bottom line: the hours in this lesson are the long-standing structure, but they are in flux. <span class="hl">Check your broker and the exchanges for current hours and which stocks are eligible.</span> Overnight sessions share the same risks as extended hours, often even thinner.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Markets that never sleep', html: `<p>Stocks are not the only game in town, and other markets keep very different hours.</p><ul><li><strong>Futures</strong> (contracts on things like the S&amp;P 500, oil, and gold) trade nearly 24 hours a day on electronic platforms. Many popular contracts on CME Globex run from Sunday 6:00pm ET to Friday 5:00pm ET, with a short daily break, typically 5:00 to 6:00pm ET.</li><li><strong>Foreign exchange (forex)</strong> trades around the clock on weekdays as markets hand off from Asia to Europe to the Americas.</li><li><strong>Crypto</strong> trades 24/7, including weekends and holidays. There is no closing bell at all.</li></ul><p>This is why you can wake up and see that "stock futures are down" before the stock market opens. Futures react overnight to news from Asia and Europe, giving a preview of how the US open might look. It is a hint, not a guarantee.</p>` },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Market clock vocabulary', cards: [ { front: 'Regular session', back: '9:30am to 4:00pm ET, Monday to Friday.' }, { front: 'Pre-market', back: 'Trading before the open, starting as early as 4:00am ET at many brokers.' }, { front: 'After-hours', back: 'Trading after the close, typically 4:00pm to 8:00pm ET.' }, { front: 'Early close', back: 'A shortened day ending at 1:00pm ET, like the day after Thanksgiving.' }, { front: '24/5 trading', back: 'Trading around the clock on weekdays, offered by some brokers for select stocks.' } ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'Maya lives in California (Pacific Time, 3 hours behind Eastern). When does the regular session open for her?', options: ['9:30am', '12:30pm', '6:30am', '7:00am'], answer: 2, explain: '9:30am Eastern minus 3 hours is 6:30am Pacific.' },
          { q: 'Which of these days is the US stock market normally CLOSED?', options: ['Good Friday', 'Columbus Day', 'Veterans Day', 'The day after Thanksgiving'], answer: 0, explain: 'Good Friday is a market holiday even though it is not a federal holiday. Columbus Day and Veterans Day are normal trading days, and the day after Thanksgiving is an early close.' },
          { q: 'What is a typical after-hours window for US stocks?', options: ['8:00pm to midnight ET', '4:00pm to 8:00pm ET', '12:00pm to 4:00pm ET', '4:00am to 9:30am ET'], answer: 1, explain: 'After-hours usually runs from the 4:00pm close to 8:00pm ET, though some brokers offer a shorter window.' },
          { q: 'A company reports earnings at 4:05pm ET. When does the full market get to react in the regular session?', options: ['Immediately at 4:05pm', 'Only next week', 'At the next day\'s 9:30am open', 'Never; earnings do not move prices'], answer: 2, explain: 'Extended-hours traders react first, but the regular session, with full liquidity, starts at the next 9:30am open.' },
          { q: 'Which market trades 24 hours a day, 7 days a week?', options: ['NYSE stocks', 'S&P 500 futures', 'Treasury bonds', 'Bitcoin and other crypto'], answer: 3, explain: 'Crypto never closes. Futures trade nearly 24 hours on weekdays but stop for the weekend.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Lesson 3: Bid, ask, spread, market makers
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    {
      id: 'money-4-bid-ask-spread',
      title: 'Bid, Ask, and the Spread',
      summary: 'Why every stock has two prices, what market makers do, and how liquidity decides what you really pay.',
      minutes: 13,
      icon: 'scale',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Every stock has two prices', html: `<p>Look up a stock and you see one price. Look closer and there are always <strong>two</strong>:</p><ul><li>The <span class="term" data-def="The highest price a buyer is currently willing to pay.">bid</span>: the highest price someone is willing to pay right now.</li><li>The <span class="term" data-def="The lowest price a seller is currently willing to accept. Also called the offer.">ask</span> (or offer): the lowest price someone is willing to sell for right now.</li></ul><p>The gap between them is the <span class="term" data-def="The difference between the ask and the bid.">spread</span>.</p><p>Think of a used-sneaker shop. The shop will buy your sneakers for $80 (their bid) and sell the same pair for $100 (their ask). The $20 gap is how they make money. The stock market works the same way, just with gaps measured in pennies for popular stocks.</p><p>The "price" you see in a headline is usually the <strong>last trade</strong>, which may sit at the bid, at the ask, or in between.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: "Buy at the market and you usually pay the ask. Sell at the market and you usually get the bid. The spread is the toll in between." } },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'order-book', caption: 'The order book: buyers (bids) stacked below, sellers (asks) stacked above. The gap between the top bid and the lowest ask is the spread. Notice how the sizes at each price show how deep the market is.' },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'A stock has a bid of $50.00 and an ask of $50.04. What is the spread in dollars?', answer: 0.04, tolerance: 0.001, unit: '$', explain: '$50.04 - $50.00 = $0.04 per share. Buy and immediately sell 100 shares at market and you lose about $4 to the spread.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The order book', html: `<p>Behind the bid and ask is an <span class="term" data-def="The list of all waiting buy and sell orders for a security, sorted by price.">order book</span>: a list of everyone waiting to trade, sorted by price.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Bids (buyers)</th><th>Asks (sellers)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>300 @ $24.98</td><td>200 @ $25.01</td></tr><tr><td>500 @ $24.97</td><td>400 @ $25.02</td></tr><tr><td>1,000 @ $24.95</td><td>800 @ $25.05</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Best bid: $24.98. Best ask: $25.01. Spread: 3 cents.</p><p>If Leo sends a market order to buy 100 shares, he fills at $25.01. If he wants 500 shares, the first 200 fill at $25.01, then the next 300 at $25.02. Big orders "walk the book," eating through price levels. The quantity at each level is called <strong>depth</strong>, and it tells you how much you can trade before the price moves.</p>` },
        { type: 'widget', name: 'bid-ask', props: {}, caption: 'A mini order book. Place a market order and watch it fill at the ask (or bid), then try a limit order that waits in the book until someone meets your price.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Market makers: the always-open shop', html: `<p>Who is quoting all those bids and asks? A lot of it comes from <span class="term" data-def="A firm that continuously offers to both buy and sell a security, earning money from the spread.">market makers</span>. These firms post prices to buy and sell at the same time, all day long.</p><p>Their business is the spread. Buy at $24.98, sell at $25.01, pocket 3 cents. Do that millions of times a day and it adds up. The risk is getting stuck holding shares when the price moves against them, so they use fast computers and hedge constantly.</p><p>Why are they useful? Without market makers, if you wanted to sell at 11:13am, you would have to wait for a buyer who happened to want the exact same stock at the exact same moment. Market makers fill that gap. They provide <strong>liquidity</strong>, the ability to trade quickly at a fair price, and in return they earn the spread.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'bolt', mood: 'happy', say: "Market makers quote both sides, all day, mostly by algorithm. Beep. Buy at the bid, sell at the ask, repeat a few million times." } },
        { type: 'check', question: 'How do market makers mainly make money?', options: ['Charging a monthly subscription', 'Earning the spread between their bid and ask prices', 'Collecting dividends from every stock', 'Receiving a fee from the SEC'], answer: 1, explain: 'They buy at the bid and sell at the ask over and over. The spread is their compensation for providing liquidity and taking inventory risk.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Liquidity decides the spread', html: `<p>Spreads are not the same for every stock.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Stock type</th><th>Typical spread</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Giant, heavily traded company or big index ETF</td><td>Often one penny</td></tr><tr><td>Mid-sized company</td><td>A few cents</td></tr><tr><td>Tiny, rarely traded stock</td><td>Can be 1% or more of the price</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The more people trade something, the tighter the spread, because lots of buyers and sellers compete to offer the best price. A heavily traded stock is called <strong>liquid</strong>. A thinly traded one is <strong>illiquid</strong>.</p><p>Spreads also widen when conditions get scary or quiet: in extended hours, during breaking news, and in market panics. That is when market makers face the most risk, so they demand a bigger cushion.</p><p>For long-term investors buying a big index fund, the spread is usually a tiny cost. For someone trading in and out of small stocks all day, it can quietly eat most of the profits.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "A 1% spread on a tiny stock means you start every trade 1% in the hole. Trade it ten times and that adds up fast." } },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'fact', title: 'The midpoint', html: `<p>Halfway between the bid and the ask is the <strong>midpoint</strong>. With a bid of $25.00 and an ask of $25.04, the midpoint is $25.02. Sometimes your order fills <em>better</em> than the quoted ask, say at $25.03 or even $25.02. That is called <span class="term" data-def="Getting a better price than the best quoted bid or ask at the time of your order.">price improvement</span>, and brokers often advertise how much of it they get for customers. You will see why that matters in the life-of-a-trade lesson.</p>` },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'If you buy a stock with a market order and immediately sell it with a market order, you will usually lose about the spread, even if the price did not move.', answer: true, explain: 'You buy at the ask and sell at the bid. The difference between them is a real cost every time you make a round trip.' },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'example', title: 'Dev pays the toll', html: `<p>Dev trades a small stock with a bid of $4.00 and an ask of $4.10. He buys 1,000 shares at $4.10 ($4,100). The price does not change. He gets bored and sells at the bid, $4.00 ($4,000). He just lost <span class="down">$100</span>, about 2.4%, on a stock that did not move. Do that a few times a week and the spread becomes his biggest expense.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Reading a quote like a pro', html: `<p>Most broker apps show a quote like this:</p><p><strong>Bid 182.40 x 300 | Ask 182.42 x 500</strong></p><p>Read it as: buyers want 300 shares at $182.40, and sellers offer 500 shares at $182.42. Some apps show the size in round lots of 100 shares, so "3 x 5" might mean 300 and 500.</p><p>Quick checklist before you trade:</p><ol><li><strong>How wide is the spread?</strong> As a percentage of the price, not just in cents.</li><li><strong>How much size is there?</strong> If you want 1,000 shares and only 100 are offered, expect to walk the book.</li><li><strong>What time is it?</strong> Spreads are usually tightest in the middle of the regular session and widest in extended hours.</li></ol><p>A wide spread is the market telling you it is hard to trade this thing right now. Listen to it.</p>` },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Spread vocabulary', cards: [ { front: 'Bid', back: 'Highest price a buyer will pay right now.' }, { front: 'Ask', back: 'Lowest price a seller will accept right now.' }, { front: 'Spread', back: 'The gap between the ask and the bid.' }, { front: 'Market maker', back: 'A firm that quotes both bids and asks and earns the spread.' }, { front: 'Depth', back: 'How many shares are waiting at each price level.' } ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'A quote shows Bid $10.00 and Ask $10.05. If you buy with a market order, about what price do you pay?', options: ['$10.00', '$10.025', '$10.05', '$9.95'], answer: 2, explain: 'A market buy order takes the best available ask, here $10.05.' },
          { q: 'Which stock is likely to have the tightest spread?', options: ['A huge company traded millions of times a day', 'A tiny company traded a few times a day', 'Any stock in after-hours trading', 'A stock during a trading halt'], answer: 0, explain: 'Heavy trading means lots of competing buyers and sellers, which squeezes the spread down, often to a penny.' },
          { q: 'What does "walking the book" mean?', options: ['Reading every order slowly', 'A large order filling at several progressively worse price levels', 'Canceling all your orders', 'Trading only at the closing auction'], answer: 1, explain: 'If your order is bigger than the shares available at the best price, it keeps filling at the next levels.' },
          { q: 'When do spreads usually widen?', options: ['Midday in the regular session for big stocks', 'Never; spreads are fixed', 'Only on Mondays', 'In extended hours and during scary news'], answer: 3, explain: 'Fewer traders or more uncertainty means market makers demand a bigger cushion for the risk.' },
          { q: 'Why are market makers useful to regular investors?', options: ['They guarantee profits', 'They let you trade quickly by always quoting a bid and an ask', 'They set the official stock price by law', 'They pay your taxes'], answer: 1, explain: 'They provide liquidity, so you rarely have to wait for a matching investor to show up.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Lesson 4: Order types
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    {
      id: 'money-4-order-types',
      title: 'Order Types: Telling Your Broker Exactly What You Want',
      summary: 'Market, limit, stop, stop-limit, and trailing stop orders, plus time-in-force, slippage, and gaps.',
      minutes: 15,
      icon: 'target',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'The market order: right now, please', html: `<p>When you tap Buy and do not change any settings, you are usually sending a <span class="term" data-def="An order to buy or sell immediately at the best available price.">market order</span>. It says: "Fill me now at the best price available."</p><p>Market orders give you <strong>certainty of execution</strong>. In a busy stock during regular hours, you will be filled within a moment, usually very close to the price you saw.</p><p>What they do <em>not</em> give you is <strong>certainty of price</strong>. You get whatever the market offers at that instant. In a liquid stock at 11am, that is fine. In a thin stock, a fast-moving market, or right at the open, the price you get can be noticeably worse than the price on your screen.</p><p>Rule of thumb: market orders are reasonable for small orders in heavily traded stocks and ETFs during regular hours. Everywhere else, think twice.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'bolt', mood: 'think', say: "Market order received. Executing at the best available price. Note: I did not promise WHICH price. That is on you." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The limit order: my price or nothing', html: `<p>A <span class="term" data-def="An order to buy at or below, or sell at or above, a price you set.">limit order</span> sets the worst price you will accept.</p><ul><li><strong>Buy limit</strong> at $48: "Buy at $48 or lower. Never pay more."</li><li><strong>Sell limit</strong> at $55: "Sell at $55 or higher. Never take less."</li></ul><p>Limit orders give you <strong>certainty of price</strong> but not of execution. If the stock never trades down to $48, your buy order just sits there unfilled.</p><p>A limit order placed right at or through the current price, like a buy limit at the current ask, usually fills immediately, just with a safety cap. Traders call that a marketable limit order. It is a popular way to get the speed of a market order without the risk of a surprise price.</p><p>Maya uses limit orders for nearly everything. Worst case, she misses a trade. That beats overpaying by 5% because the price spiked for a few seconds.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: 'A stock\'s ask is $50.00. Maya places a buy limit order at $48.00. What happens?', options: ['It fills right away at $50.00', 'It fills right away at $48.00', 'It waits, and fills only if sellers offer shares at $48.00 or lower', 'It is rejected because the price is too low'], answer: 2, explain: 'A buy limit below the current ask rests in the order book. It only executes if the price comes down to her limit or better.' },
        { type: 'widget', name: 'bid-ask', props: {}, caption: 'Practice here: send a market order and see where it fills, then set a buy limit below the ask and watch it wait in the book.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Stop orders: the trip wire', html: `<p>A <span class="term" data-def="An order that becomes a market order once the stock trades at or through a trigger price you set.">stop order</span> (often called a stop-loss) sits dormant until the price hits a trigger. Then it turns into a <strong>market order</strong>.</p><p>Leo buys a stock at $40 and sets a sell stop at $36. If the stock trades down to $36, the stop triggers and his shares are sold at the next available price. The idea is to cap his loss around 10%.</p><p>Here is the catch: <span class="hl">a stop is not a guaranteed price</span>. Say the stock closes at $39, then bad news comes out overnight and it opens at $31. His stop triggers at the open and sells around $31, well below $36. That jump over his trigger is called a <strong>gap</strong>.</p><p>Stops can also trigger on a brief, wild dip, selling you right before the price bounces back. And many brokers do not trigger stops outside regular hours.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "A stop-loss is a seatbelt, not a force field. If the price gaps past your stop, you get filled wherever the market is." } },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Leo bought at $40 with a sell stop at $36. The stock gaps down and his stop fills at $31. How many dollars per share did he lose from his purchase price?', answer: 9, tolerance: 0, unit: '$', explain: '$40 - $31 = $9 per share, much more than the $4 he planned. The stop became a market order and filled at the first available price after the gap.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Stop-limit and trailing stops', html: `<p>A <strong>stop-limit order</strong> has two prices: a stop to trigger it and a limit for the worst price. Leo could set "stop $36, limit $35." When the stock hits $36, it becomes a limit order to sell at $35 or better.</p><p>Upside: he will never sell for less than $35. Downside: if the stock gaps to $31, his order does not fill at all, and he is still holding as it keeps falling. You trade gap risk for the risk of not getting out.</p><p>A <strong>trailing stop</strong> follows the price up. Set a 10% trailing stop on a stock bought at $100: the stop starts at $90. If the stock climbs to $130, the stop rises to $117. It never moves back down. If the price then falls to $117, the order triggers. It is a way to lock in some gains while still giving the stock room to run.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Maya sets a 10% trailing stop. The stock rises to a high of $80 and then starts falling. At what price does her stop trigger?', answer: 72, tolerance: 0, unit: '$', explain: 'The trailing stop sits 10% below the highest price reached. 10% of $80 is $8, so the stop triggers at $72.' },
        { type: 'match', prompt: 'Match each order type to what it does.', pairs: [ { left: 'Market', right: 'Fills immediately at the best available price' }, { left: 'Limit', right: 'Fills only at your price or better' }, { left: 'Stop', right: 'Becomes a market order when a trigger price is hit' }, { left: 'Stop-limit', right: 'Becomes a limit order when a trigger price is hit' }, { left: 'Trailing stop', right: 'Trigger price moves up as the stock rises' } ] },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Time in force: how long should it wait?', html: `<p>Every order also has a <span class="term" data-def="An instruction for how long an order stays active before it is canceled.">time-in-force</span> setting:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Day</td><td>Expires at the end of today's session if not filled. Usually the default.</td></tr><tr><td>GTC (good till canceled)</td><td>Stays open across days until filled or canceled. Many brokers auto-cancel after a set period, often 60 to 180 days.</td></tr><tr><td>IOC (immediate or cancel)</td><td>Fill whatever you can right now and cancel the rest.</td></tr><tr><td>FOK (fill or kill)</td><td>Fill the entire order right now, or cancel all of it.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Some brokers add options like "day plus extended hours." Forgotten GTC orders are a classic beginner trap. Dev once set a GTC buy limit far below the price, forgot about it, and it filled three months later during a crash he had not been following.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Slippage and why extended hours need limits', html: `<p><span class="term" data-def="The difference between the price you expected and the price you actually got.">Slippage</span> is the gap between the price you expected and the price you got. It comes from:</p><ul><li><strong>Fast markets</strong>: the price moves between your tap and your fill.</li><li><strong>Thin liquidity</strong>: few shares at the best price, so your order walks the book.</li><li><strong>Big orders</strong>: more shares than the top of the book can absorb.</li><li><strong>Gaps</strong>: the price jumps past your stop overnight or on news.</li></ul><p>This is exactly why most brokers require limit orders in pre-market, after-hours, and overnight sessions. With fewer traders around, a market order could fill far from the last price. The limit is your guardrail.</p><p>Simple habits cut slippage: trade liquid stocks, avoid the first few minutes after the open when prices are chaotic, use limit orders, and break big orders into smaller ones.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: "The first few minutes after 9:30 are often the most chaotic of the day. Spreads can be wider while the market finds its footing." } },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'A stop-loss order at $45 guarantees you will sell at $45.', answer: false, explain: 'A stop becomes a market order when triggered. If the price gaps below $45, you get the next available price, which could be much lower.' },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Order vocabulary', cards: [ { front: 'Market order', back: 'Fill now at the best available price; certain to fill, price not guaranteed.' }, { front: 'Limit order', back: 'Fill only at your price or better; price guaranteed, fill not guaranteed.' }, { front: 'Stop order', back: 'Turns into a market order when a trigger price trades.' }, { front: 'GTC', back: 'Good till canceled: stays open across days until filled or canceled.' }, { front: 'Gap', back: 'A jump in price between one trade and the next, often overnight.' } ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'Which order guarantees your price but not that it fills?', options: ['Market order', 'Stop order', 'Limit order', 'Trailing stop'], answer: 2, explain: 'A limit order only executes at your price or better, so it might never fill.' },
          { q: 'A stop-limit order with stop $36 and limit $35 is triggered, but the stock gaps to $31. What happens?', options: ['It sells at $36', 'It sells at $35', 'It sells at $31', 'It does not fill unless the price comes back to $35 or higher'], answer: 3, explain: 'Once triggered, it becomes a sell limit at $35. With the price at $31, no one will pay $35, so it sits unfilled.' },
          { q: 'What does a fill-or-kill (FOK) order require?', options: ['The entire order fills immediately or it is canceled', 'It fills by the end of the day', 'It stays open for 90 days', 'Only part needs to fill'], answer: 0, explain: 'FOK is all or nothing, right now. IOC is the one that allows a partial fill.' },
          { q: 'Why do most brokers require limit orders in extended hours?', options: ['Market orders cost more commission at night', 'Low liquidity and wide spreads could cause a terrible fill', 'The SEC bans trading after 4pm', 'Limit orders settle faster'], answer: 1, explain: 'With few participants, a market order could fill far from the last price. The limit caps that risk.' },
          { q: 'Leo has a 15% trailing stop. The stock peaks at $200. Where does his stop sit?', options: ['$185', '$170', '$230', '$150'], answer: 1, explain: '15% of $200 is $30, so the stop trails at $170.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Lesson 5: Life of a trade
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    {
      id: 'money-4-life-of-a-trade',
      title: 'The Life of a Trade',
      summary: 'From tap to settled: routing, payment for order flow, the NBBO, clearing, T+1 settlement, fractional shares, and cash vs margin accounts.',
      minutes: 15,
      icon: 'bolt',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'One second, a lot of hands', html: `<p>It is 10:15am. Maya taps Buy on $100 of an S&amp;P 500 ETF. Half a second later, the app says "Filled." It feels like nothing happened. Actually, a small army just did its job:</p><ol><li><strong>Your broker</strong> checks you have the money and decides where to send the order.</li><li><strong>A trading venue</strong> (an exchange, a market maker, or an ATS) matches it with a seller.</li><li><strong>A clearinghouse</strong> steps in to guarantee both sides follow through.</li><li><strong>Settlement</strong> makes the swap of cash for shares official, one business day later.</li></ol><p>Steps 1 and 2 take a fraction of a second. Steps 3 and 4 happen behind the scenes. Knowing the whole chain helps you understand "free" trading, why your sale cash is not instantly withdrawable, and what can go wrong.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'wow', say: "You tapped once. Behind that tap: a broker, a market maker or exchange, a clearinghouse, and a settlement system. Teamwork." } },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'trade-lifecycle', caption: 'Follow an order from you to your broker, out to a market maker or exchange, then through clearing and settlement one business day later (T+1).' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Routing and payment for order flow', html: `<p>Your broker decides where your order goes. Options include an exchange, an ATS, or a <strong>wholesaler</strong>: a big market maker that fills retail orders itself.</p><p>Many US brokers that offer $0 commissions send lots of retail orders to wholesalers, and the wholesalers pay the broker for them. That is <span class="term" data-def="Payment a broker receives from a market maker in exchange for sending it customer orders.">payment for order flow (PFOF)</span>.</p><p>Why pay for orders? Retail orders are mostly small and not based on secret information, so they are less risky for the market maker to trade against. The wholesaler earns money from the spread and shares part of it with the broker.</p><p>Supporters say PFOF funds free trading and wholesalers often fill orders at slightly better prices than the public quote. Critics say it creates a conflict of interest: is your broker routing for your best price or for its biggest check? The practice is legal in the US but must be disclosed; brokers publish quarterly routing reports. Some other countries, such as the UK, have banned it.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The NBBO and best execution', html: `<p>With more than a dozen exchanges, which price is "the" price? The answer is the <span class="term" data-def="National best bid and offer: the highest bid and lowest ask across all US exchanges at a given moment.">NBBO</span>, the national best bid and offer. It combines quotes from every exchange into one best bid and one best ask.</p><p>US market rules generally prevent your order from being filled at a price worse than the best displayed quotes on exchanges. On top of that, brokers have a legal duty of <strong>best execution</strong>: they must use reasonable diligence to get you the most favorable terms reasonably available, considering price, speed, and likelihood of a fill.</p><p>Often you even get <strong>price improvement</strong>: a fill slightly better than the NBBO. If the NBBO ask is $50.02 and you buy at $50.015, you saved half a cent per share. Tiny per trade, but brokers compete on these stats and publish them.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: "NBBO stands for national best bid and offer. It is the market's scoreboard, stitched together from every exchange at once." } },
        { type: 'check', question: 'What is the NBBO?', options: ['A fee charged by the New York Stock Exchange', 'The highest bid and lowest ask across all US exchanges at that moment', 'The average price of a stock over the day', 'A government bond'], answer: 1, explain: 'The NBBO is the best available bid and ask nationwide. Fills are generally supposed to be at that price or better.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Clearing and settlement', html: `<p>Your trade <em>executes</em> in a fraction of a second, but it is not officially finished. Two more steps:</p><p><strong>Clearing.</strong> The National Securities Clearing Corporation (NSCC), part of the Depository Trust &amp; Clearing Corporation (DTCC), sits in the middle of most US stock trades. It becomes the buyer to every seller and the seller to every buyer, so if one broker fails, the other side still gets paid. It also nets trades together, so firms only move the difference instead of every single trade.</p><p><strong>Settlement.</strong> This is when cash and shares officially swap owners. Since <strong>May 28, 2024</strong>, most US stock trades settle <span class="hl">T+1</span>: one business day after the trade date. Before that it was T+2, and before 2017 it was T+3. Faster settlement means less time for things to go wrong between trade and payment.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: 'Leo sells shares on a Friday that is not a holiday. Under T+1 settlement, when does the trade settle?', options: ['The same Friday', 'Saturday', 'The following Monday', 'The following Wednesday'], answer: 2, explain: 'T+1 means one business day after the trade. Weekends are not business days, so a Friday trade settles Monday (or Tuesday if Monday is a market holiday).' },
        { type: 'order', prompt: 'Put the life of Maya\'s trade in order.', items: ['Maya taps Buy in her app', 'Her broker checks her cash and routes the order', 'A market maker or exchange executes the trade', 'The clearinghouse guarantees and nets the trade', 'The trade settles one business day later'], explain: 'Order, routing, execution, clearing, settlement. The first three happen in a blink; the last two run behind the scenes.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Fractional shares', html: `<p>One share of some companies costs hundreds or even thousands of dollars. <strong>Fractional shares</strong> let you buy by dollar amount instead: $25 of a $500 stock gets you 0.05 of a share.</p><p>Your broker handles the splitting, often by owning whole shares and tracking customer slices. You still get your proportional share of price changes and dividends.</p><p>A few fine-print items:</p><ul><li>Not every stock or ETF is available in fractions at every broker.</li><li>Fractional orders may only be filled at certain times or as market orders.</li><li>Fractional shares may not transfer to another broker; you might have to sell them first.</li><li>Voting rights for fractional shares vary by broker.</li></ul><p>For a teen investing $20 a week, fractions are a big deal. Maya can buy a slice of an expensive index ETF every payday instead of saving for weeks to afford one whole share.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Cash accounts vs margin accounts', html: `<p>In a <strong>cash account</strong>, you buy with money you actually have. Simple. Your max loss is what you put in.</p><p>In a <strong>margin account</strong>, the broker can lend you money to buy more, using your investments as collateral. Under long-standing Federal Reserve rules, you can typically borrow up to 50% of the purchase price of a stock. You also have to keep a minimum level of equity, at least 25% under FINRA rules and often higher at many brokers.</p><p>If your investments drop and your equity falls below the minimum, you get a <span class="term" data-def="A demand from your broker to add cash or securities, or else it may sell your holdings.">margin call</span>. Add money fast, or the broker can sell your holdings, without asking, at whatever the price is. And you pay interest on the loan the whole time.</p><p>Margin magnifies gains and losses. With 2x margin, a 50% drop wipes out your entire stake. Margin accounts generally require you to be 18 and get approved.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "Margin lets you lose borrowed money too. A 50% drop on 2x margin wipes out everything you put in. Beginners: cash account." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Good-faith violations', html: `<p>Cash accounts have a trap tied to settlement. When you sell, the cash is not settled until T+1. Many brokers let you buy something new with that unsettled cash right away. The problem comes if you then <em>sell</em> the new purchase before the original cash has settled.</p><p>Example: Monday morning, Dev sells Stock A for $1,000. That cash settles Tuesday. Monday at noon he uses the $1,000 to buy Stock B. Monday afternoon he sells Stock B. He just bought and sold B without ever paying for it with settled money. That is a <span class="term" data-def="Selling a security in a cash account before the money used to buy it has settled.">good-faith violation</span>.</p><p>If he had waited until Tuesday to sell B, there would be no violation. Consequences depend on the broker, but a common policy is that a few violations within 12 months gets your account restricted to settled cash only for 90 days.</p>` },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'In a cash account, using unsettled sale proceeds to buy a stock, then selling that stock before the original proceeds settle, can trigger a good-faith violation.', answer: true, explain: 'The new position was never fully paid for with settled funds. Waiting until the original sale settles avoids the violation.' },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Trade plumbing vocabulary', cards: [ { front: 'PFOF', back: 'Payment for order flow: market makers paying brokers to route customer orders to them.' }, { front: 'NBBO', back: 'The best bid and best ask across all US exchanges right now.' }, { front: 'Best execution', back: 'A broker\'s duty to seek the most favorable terms reasonably available for your order.' }, { front: 'T+1', back: 'Settlement one business day after the trade date; the US standard since May 28, 2024.' }, { front: 'Margin call', back: 'A demand to add money when borrowed-money losses push your equity too low.' } ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'What is payment for order flow?', options: ['A tax on every trade', 'A market maker paying a broker to route customer orders to it', 'A fee you pay the exchange', 'Interest on a margin loan'], answer: 1, explain: 'Wholesalers pay brokers for retail orders. It helps fund $0 commissions but raises conflict-of-interest questions.' },
          { q: 'When did the US move stock settlement from T+2 to T+1?', options: ['January 2020', 'May 2024', 'March 2017', 'October 2008'], answer: 1, explain: 'T+1 took effect on May 28, 2024. The earlier move from T+3 to T+2 happened in 2017.' },
          { q: 'What role does the NSCC, part of DTCC, play?', options: ['It sets stock prices', 'It regulates insider trading', 'It runs your broker app', 'It acts as the central counterparty that guarantees and nets trades'], answer: 3, explain: 'As central counterparty, it stands between buyers and sellers so one failing firm does not sink the other side.' },
          { q: 'Under long-standing Federal Reserve rules, how much of a stock purchase can you typically borrow on margin at first?', options: ['Up to 50%', 'Up to 10%', 'Up to 90%', 'Up to 100%'], answer: 0, explain: 'The long-standing initial margin requirement for stocks is 50%. Brokers can require more.' },
          { q: 'Maya wants $30 of a stock that costs $600 per share. What lets her do that?', options: ['A margin loan', 'A stop order', 'Fractional shares', 'A dark pool'], answer: 2, explain: 'Fractional shares let her buy by dollar amount: $30 / $600 = 0.05 of a share.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Lesson 6: Indexes and market moods
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    {
      id: 'money-4-indexes-and-moods',
      title: 'Indexes, Bulls, Bears, and Circuit Breakers',
      summary: 'The S&P 500, Dow, Nasdaq, Russell 2000, and VIX, plus what bull markets, bear markets, corrections, and circuit breakers mean.',
      minutes: 15,
      icon: 'bull',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'An index is a scoreboard', html: `<p>When the news says "the market rose 1% today," which market? Thousands of stocks moved in different directions. To summarize, we use an <span class="term" data-def="A measurement of a group of stocks, used to track how that slice of the market is doing.">index</span>: a single number that tracks a group of stocks.</p><p>Think of it as the scoreboard for a slice of the market. It does not tell you how every player did, but it tells you who is winning.</p><p>Indexes are built differently. Some include a few dozen stocks, some thousands. Some give bigger companies more weight, and one famous one gives more weight to stocks with higher share prices, which is as odd as it sounds.</p><p>Indexes also matter because trillions of dollars in index funds copy them. When a company is added to a major index, funds tracking that index have to buy it.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'happy', say: "When the news says 'stocks rallied today,' they usually mean the S&P 500 went up. It is the scoreboard most pros watch." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The big indexes', html: `<ul><li><strong>S&amp;P 500</strong>: about 500 large US companies chosen by a committee at S&amp;P Dow Jones Indices. Weighted by market value, so giant companies count more. S&amp;P says it covers roughly 80% of the total value of US stocks, which is why it is the go-to benchmark for "the market."</li><li><strong>Nasdaq Composite</strong>: nearly every stock listed on the Nasdaq exchange, thousands of them. Heavy in technology.</li><li><strong>Nasdaq-100</strong>: about 100 of the largest non-financial companies listed on Nasdaq. Popular ETFs track it.</li><li><strong>Russell 2000</strong>: about 2,000 small US companies. The usual scoreboard for small caps.</li><li><strong>Dow Jones Industrial Average</strong>: just 30 big, well-known US companies. The oldest of the bunch, dating back to 1896.</li></ul><p>On most days they move in the same direction. When they split, it tells a story: if the Russell 2000 falls while the Nasdaq-100 rises, small companies are struggling while big tech is doing fine.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The Dow is weird', html: `<p>The Dow is <span class="term" data-def="An index where stocks with higher share prices have more influence, regardless of company size.">price-weighted</span>. A stock's influence depends on its <em>share price</em>, not how big the company is.</p><p>So a $400 stock moving 1% (a $4 change) moves the Dow eight times as much as a $50 stock moving 1% (a $0.50 change), even if the $50 company is much larger. Most modern indexes weight by company value instead, which is why many pros prefer the S&amp;P 500.</p><p>Also watch the units. Headlines love "Dow plunges 500 points!" Points sound dramatic, but percent is what matters. When the Dow is around 40,000, 500 points is only about 1.25%. Back when it was 1,000, 500 points would have been a catastrophe.</p><p>Always convert points to percent before you panic.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'wow', say: "In the Dow, a stock's share price decides its weight. Company size does not. It is a 19th-century design that stuck around." } },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'The Dow is at 40,000 and falls 600 points. What percentage drop is that?', answer: 1.5, tolerance: 0.05, unit: '%', explain: '600 / 40,000 = 0.015, or 1.5%. A big-sounding point number can be a fairly ordinary percentage move.' },
        { type: 'match', prompt: 'Match each index to what it tracks.', pairs: [ { left: 'S&P 500', right: 'About 500 large US companies, weighted by value' }, { left: 'Dow Jones Industrial Average', right: '30 big US companies, weighted by share price' }, { left: 'Russell 2000', right: 'About 2,000 small US companies' }, { left: 'Nasdaq-100', right: 'About 100 large non-financial Nasdaq-listed companies' }, { left: 'VIX', right: 'Expected S&P 500 volatility over the next 30 days' } ] },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The VIX: the fear gauge', html: `<p>The <span class="term" data-def="Cboe Volatility Index: a measure of expected S&amp;P 500 volatility over the next 30 days, based on option prices.">VIX</span> does not track prices. It tracks <em>expected swings</em>. Cboe calculates it from the prices of S&amp;P 500 options, which show how much protection traders are paying for over the next 30 days.</p><p>Calm markets usually mean a low VIX. Panic means a high VIX, because everyone rushes to buy insurance. During the 2008 financial crisis and again in March 2020, the VIX spiked above 80, while in quiet stretches it has often sat in the teens.</p><p>That is why people call it the "fear gauge." When the VIX jumps, stocks are usually falling.</p><p>You cannot buy the VIX itself. Products tied to it are built on VIX futures and can behave very differently from the index, sometimes losing value steadily over time. They are tools for professionals, not long-term holdings.</p>` },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'bull-bear', caption: 'A bull market climbs over time with dips along the way. A bear market is a drop of about 20% or more from a recent high. Notice that even bull markets contain scary pullbacks.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Bulls, bears, and corrections', html: `<p>Market moods get animal names. A bull thrusts its horns <em>up</em>. A bear swipes its paws <em>down</em>.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Rule of thumb</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pullback</td><td>A dip of around 5% or so</td></tr><tr><td>Correction</td><td>A drop of 10% or more from a recent high</td></tr><tr><td>Bear market</td><td>A drop of 20% or more from a recent high</td></tr><tr><td>Bull market</td><td>A sustained rise, often dated from a 20% gain off a low</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These are conventions, not laws. Nobody rings a bell. In 2020, the S&amp;P 500 fell into a bear market in a matter of weeks and recovered within months. In 2022, it slid about 25% from its January high to its October low over most of the year.</p><p>Corrections are common. Historically, they have happened every year or two on average. Expecting them makes them much easier to sit through.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'think', say: "Bear markets feel like they will last forever. Historically, they have been shorter than the bull markets that followed, but no promises." } },
        { type: 'check', question: 'The S&P 500 drops 13% from its all-time high and then stabilizes. What is the usual label?', options: ['A pullback', 'A correction', 'A bear market', 'A crash'], answer: 1, explain: 'A decline of 10% to 20% from a recent high is usually called a correction. At 20% or more, people start calling it a bear market.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Circuit breakers: the emergency brakes', html: `<p>On really bad days, the market has emergency brakes. <span class="term" data-def="Rules that pause or stop trading across the whole US stock market when the S&amp;P 500 falls sharply in one day.">Market-wide circuit breakers</span> are based on how far the S&amp;P 500 has fallen from the previous day's close:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Drop</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Level 1</td><td>7%</td><td>15-minute trading halt, if before 3:25pm ET</td></tr><tr><td>Level 2</td><td>13%</td><td>Another 15-minute halt, if before 3:25pm ET</td></tr><tr><td>Level 3</td><td>20%</td><td>Trading stops for the rest of the day, at any time</td></tr></tbody></table><p>If a Level 1 or 2 drop happens at or after 3:25pm, trading continues. The pause gives everyone time to breathe, absorb news, and stop panic from feeding on itself.</p><p>They have been triggered in real life: Level 1 halts hit four times in March 2020. Individual stocks also have their own pause rules, called limit up-limit down, which can halt a single stock for a few minutes after a very sharp move.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'The S&P 500 closed yesterday at 5,000. At roughly what index level would a Level 1 circuit breaker (7% drop) trigger today?', answer: 4650, tolerance: 1, unit: '', explain: '7% of 5,000 is 350. 5,000 - 350 = 4,650.' },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'A Level 3 circuit breaker, a 20% drop in the S&P 500, halts US stock trading for the rest of the day.', answer: true, explain: 'Level 3 ends trading for the day no matter what time it triggers. Levels 1 and 2 cause 15-minute pauses only if they hit before 3:25pm ET.' },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Index and mood vocabulary', cards: [ { front: 'Market-cap weighted', back: 'Bigger companies have more influence on the index.' }, { front: 'Price-weighted', back: 'Higher-priced shares have more influence, like in the Dow.' }, { front: 'Correction', back: 'A drop of 10% or more from a recent high.' }, { front: 'Bear market', back: 'A drop of 20% or more from a recent high.' }, { front: 'Circuit breaker', back: 'A market-wide trading halt triggered by a sharp S&P 500 drop.' } ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'Why can a cheaper share price give a stock less influence in the Dow, even if the company is huge?', options: ['The Dow is price-weighted', 'The Dow only includes small companies', 'The Dow excludes tech stocks', 'The Dow is weighted by dividends'], answer: 0, explain: 'In a price-weighted index, a higher share price means more weight, regardless of company size.' },
          { q: 'Which index is the usual benchmark for small US companies?', options: ['Nasdaq-100', 'Dow Jones Industrial Average', 'Russell 2000', 'VIX'], answer: 2, explain: 'The Russell 2000 tracks about 2,000 small-cap US stocks.' },
          { q: 'What does a high VIX usually signal?', options: ['Low trading volume', 'Rising interest rates', 'Strong earnings', 'Traders expect big swings, often during fear'], answer: 3, explain: 'The VIX reflects option prices. When fear rises, traders pay up for protection and the VIX climbs.' },
          { q: 'At 2:00pm ET the S&P 500 is down 7% from yesterday\'s close. What happens?', options: ['Nothing; only 20% matters', 'Trading pauses for 15 minutes', 'Trading closes for the day', 'Trading closes for a week'], answer: 1, explain: 'A 7% drop before 3:25pm ET triggers a Level 1 circuit breaker, a 15-minute market-wide halt.' },
          { q: 'Under the usual rule of thumb, what is a bear market?', options: ['Any down day', 'A 5% dip', 'A 10% drop from a recent high', 'A 20% or larger drop from a recent high'], answer: 3, explain: 'Ten percent is a correction. Twenty percent or more from a recent high is the common bear-market threshold.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Lesson 7: The referees
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    {
      id: 'money-4-the-referees',
      title: 'The Referees: SEC, FINRA, SIPC, and the Rules',
      summary: 'Who polices the market, what SIPC does and does not protect, and the rules every new trader trips over: insider trading, pattern day trading, and wash sales.',
      minutes: 15,
      icon: 'shield',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'The SEC: the main referee', html: `<p>After the 1929 crash and the Great Depression, Congress created the <span class="term" data-def="Securities and Exchange Commission: the federal agency that regulates US securities markets.">SEC</span> in 1934. Its job: protect investors, keep markets fair and orderly, and help companies raise money honestly.</p><p>What that looks like in practice:</p><ul><li><strong>Disclosure.</strong> Public companies must file regular reports, like the annual 10-K and quarterly 10-Q, so everyone gets the same key information. They are free to read on the SEC's EDGAR database.</li><li><strong>Rules</strong> for exchanges, brokers, funds, and advisers.</li><li><strong>Enforcement.</strong> The SEC investigates fraud, insider trading, and market manipulation, and brings civil cases. Criminal cases go to the Justice Department.</li></ul><p>The SEC does not tell you what to buy, and it does not promise any investment will make money. Its job is making sure the game is played with honest information.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'bolt', mood: 'happy', say: "Rule systems online. The SEC sets the rules of the game. It does not pick winners, and it does not refund bad bets." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'FINRA: the referee for brokers', html: `<p><span class="term" data-def="Financial Industry Regulatory Authority: the self-regulatory organization that oversees US broker-dealers, under SEC supervision.">FINRA</span> is a self-regulatory organization that oversees broker-dealers, the firms that handle your trades, and the people who work there. It operates under SEC oversight.</p><p>FINRA:</p><ul><li>Writes and enforces rules for how brokers treat customers, including best execution and margin requirements.</li><li>Runs the licensing exams brokers must pass.</li><li>Fines firms and bars individuals who break the rules.</li><li>Runs <strong>BrokerCheck</strong>, a free tool where you can look up any broker or firm and see its licenses, history, and disciplinary record.</li></ul><p>Before trusting anyone with your money, Ms. Ortiz looks them up on BrokerCheck. It takes two minutes. If someone selling investments is not registered anywhere, that is a giant red flag.</p>` },
        { type: 'match', prompt: 'Match each organization to its job.', pairs: [ { left: 'SEC', right: 'Federal agency that regulates securities markets' }, { left: 'FINRA', right: 'Oversees broker-dealers and runs BrokerCheck' }, { left: 'SIPC', right: 'Protects customers if a brokerage firm fails' }, { left: 'FDIC', right: 'Insures bank deposits if a bank fails' } ] },
        { type: 'read', title: 'SIPC: protection if your broker fails', html: `<p>What if your brokerage firm goes bust and customer assets are missing? That is what <span class="term" data-def="Securities Investor Protection Corporation: protects brokerage customers if their firm fails and assets are missing.">SIPC</span> is for.</p><p>SIPC protects up to <strong>$500,000</strong> per customer, including up to <strong>$250,000 in cash</strong>, when a member brokerage fails and customer securities or cash are missing. Many firms carry extra private insurance on top.</p><p>What SIPC does <em>not</em> do:</p><ul><li>It does <strong>not</strong> protect you from investment losses. If your stock falls 40%, that is the market, not a failure.</li><li>It does not cover bad advice or most investments that are not securities. Crypto at a crypto platform is generally not covered.</li></ul><p>Compare with the <strong>FDIC</strong>, which insures bank deposits up to $250,000 per depositor, per insured bank, per ownership category. Different agency, different kind of account.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'penny', mood: 'think', say: "SIPC is protection if your broker disappears, not if your stock does. FDIC is my bank-account bodyguard. Different jobs." } },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'If a stock you own drops 40%, SIPC will reimburse your loss.', answer: false, explain: 'SIPC only steps in when a brokerage firm fails and customer assets are missing. Market losses are your risk as an investor.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Insider trading', html: `<p><span class="term" data-def="Trading based on important, non-public information in violation of a duty to keep it confidential.">Insider trading</span> means buying or selling a stock based on <strong>material, non-public information</strong>, in breach of a duty to keep it confidential. "Material" means it would likely affect the price: an upcoming merger, a surprise earnings miss, a failed drug trial.</p><p>Here is the part people miss: you do not have to be an executive. Leo's friend works at a company and tells him, "We are getting bought tomorrow, buy now." If Leo trades on that tip, both the friend who leaked it and Leo can be in serious trouble. Penalties include giving back profits, big fines, and prison.</p><p>Some insider trading is legal. Executives can buy and sell their own company's stock if they follow the rules: they must report trades publicly, and many use pre-scheduled trading plans set up in advance. You can see those reports on EDGAR.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: 'Maya\'s cousin works at a drug company and tells her a trial failed, before any announcement. What should Maya do?', options: ['Sell her shares before the news comes out', 'Buy put options to profit', 'Tell her friends so they can sell too', 'Not trade on it; using that tip could be illegal insider trading'], answer: 3, explain: 'That is material, non-public information shared in breach of a duty. Trading on it, or passing it on, could make both of them liable.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The pattern day trader rule', html: `<p>A <strong>day trade</strong> means buying and selling (or selling short and buying back) the same security on the same day. For years, FINRA's <span class="term" data-def="A FINRA rule that has required at least $25,000 in a margin account for customers who make frequent day trades.">pattern day trader (PDT) rule</span> has worked like this:</p><ul><li>Make <strong>4 or more day trades within 5 business days</strong> in a margin account (if they are more than 6% of your trades in that period), and you are flagged as a pattern day trader.</li><li>Pattern day traders must keep at least <strong>$25,000</strong> of equity in the account to keep day trading.</li><li>Fall below that and day trading is restricted until you add money.</li></ul><p>Cash accounts are not covered by PDT, but they have the settled-cash limits and good-faith violations you learned about.</p><p><span class="hl">This rule has been under revision.</span> FINRA has been working on changes that could replace the $25,000 minimum with a different approach. Check FINRA and your broker for the current requirement before you rely on it.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "Whatever the PDT rule says this year, the real lesson stands: most frequent day traders lose money. Rules change. Math does not." } },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Under the long-standing PDT rule, what is the minimum account equity, in dollars, a pattern day trader must keep in a margin account?', answer: 25000, tolerance: 0, unit: '$', explain: 'The long-standing minimum has been $25,000. Because the rule has been under revision, always check the current requirement with FINRA and your broker.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The wash-sale rule', html: `<p>When you sell an investment at a loss in a taxable account, that loss can usually lower your taxes. But you cannot sell at a loss and immediately buy it back just to grab the tax break.</p><p>The <span class="term" data-def="An IRS rule that disallows a loss if you buy the same or a substantially identical security within 30 days before or after selling it at a loss.">wash-sale rule</span>: if you buy the same or a "substantially identical" security within <strong>30 days before or after</strong> selling it at a loss, you cannot claim that loss right now. That creates a 61-day window around the sale.</p><p>The loss is not gone forever in the usual case. It gets added to the cost basis of the new shares, so you get the benefit later when you sell those. But buying the replacement inside an IRA can wipe out the loss permanently.</p><p>Tax rules have details and exceptions, so a tax professional or the IRS's own guidance is the place for specifics.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: 'Maya sells a stock at a loss on June 10. What is the earliest date she can buy it back without triggering the wash-sale rule?', options: ['June 25', 'July 10', 'July 11', 'June 11'], answer: 2, explain: 'The rule covers 30 days after the sale. June 10 plus 30 days is July 10, still inside the window, so July 11 is the first safe day. She also must not have bought it in the 30 days before June 10.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Refs cannot catch everything', html: `<p>Regulators catch a lot, but often only after the damage is done. Your best protection is spotting red flags yourself:</p><ul><li><strong>Guaranteed high returns.</strong> Real investments carry risk. "Guaranteed 20% a month" is a lie.</li><li><strong>Pressure to act now.</strong> Scammers hate it when you take time to check.</li><li><strong>Unregistered sellers.</strong> Look them up on BrokerCheck and the SEC's site.</li><li><strong>Pump and dumps.</strong> Social media hype on a tiny stock, organized by people planning to sell into your buying.</li><li><strong>Returns paid from new investors' money.</strong> That is a Ponzi scheme, and it always collapses.</li></ul><p>Dev once got a direct message promising "guaranteed" crypto profits if he sent money to a private wallet. That is not investing. That is a stranger asking for your money.</p>` },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'ponzi', caption: 'In a Ponzi scheme, early investors are paid with new investors\' money, not real profits. It looks great until new money slows, then it collapses.' },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Referee vocabulary', cards: [ { front: 'SEC', back: 'The federal agency that regulates US securities markets.' }, { front: 'FINRA', back: 'The self-regulatory organization overseeing brokers; runs BrokerCheck.' }, { front: 'SIPC', back: 'Protects up to $500,000, including $250,000 cash, if a member broker fails.' }, { front: 'Insider trading', back: 'Trading on material non-public information in breach of a duty.' }, { front: 'Wash sale', back: 'Rebuying within 30 days of a loss sale, which disallows the loss for now.' } ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'What does SIPC protect against?', options: ['Losses when stock prices fall', 'Bad investment advice', 'Missing customer assets when a member brokerage fails', 'Inflation'], answer: 2, explain: 'SIPC protects up to $500,000, including $250,000 cash, if a member broker fails and assets are missing. It never covers market losses.' },
          { q: 'Where can you check a broker\'s licenses and disciplinary history?', options: ['FINRA BrokerCheck', 'The Federal Reserve website', 'The company\'s 10-K', 'The VIX'], answer: 0, explain: 'BrokerCheck is FINRA\'s free lookup tool for firms and individual brokers.' },
          { q: 'Under the long-standing PDT rule, which of these would flag Dev as a pattern day trader in a margin account?', options: ['3 day trades in 5 business days', '4 day trades in 5 business days that are over 6% of his trades', '1 day trade per month', 'Holding stocks for a year'], answer: 1, explain: 'Four or more day trades within five business days, making up more than 6% of trades, triggers the flag. The rule has been under revision, so check the current version.' },
          { q: 'Leo sells a fund at a loss and buys the exact same fund 10 days later in his taxable account. What happens?', options: ['He gets a double tax deduction', 'The SEC fines him', 'Nothing; the rule only applies to stocks', 'The wash-sale rule disallows the loss for now and adds it to his new cost basis'], answer: 3, explain: 'Rebuying within 30 days triggers a wash sale. The loss is deferred into the new shares\' cost basis.' },
          { q: 'Which is LEGAL?', options: ['Trading on a tip that a merger will be announced tomorrow', 'An executive buying company shares and reporting the trade publicly as required', 'Passing a confidential earnings number to a friend who trades', 'Spreading false rumors to push a stock up'], answer: 1, explain: 'Insiders can trade their own company\'s stock when they follow disclosure rules. The others involve non-public information or manipulation.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
  ],
  bossQuestions: [
    { q: 'At 7:30pm ET, Dev wants to buy a stock that just jumped on earnings news. Which approach is most sensible at most brokers?', options: ['A limit order, because extended hours have thin liquidity and wider spreads', 'A market order, since after-hours spreads are tight', 'A stop order, since stops work best after hours', 'Wait for the 4:00am pre-market close'], answer: 0, explain: 'After-hours trading has fewer participants and wider spreads, and most brokers require limit orders. Stops often do not trigger outside regular hours.' },
    { q: 'A stock\'s quote is Bid $20.00 x 200, Ask $20.06 x 100, with the next ask at $20.10 x 500. Leo sends a market order for 300 shares. About what average price does he pay?', options: ['$20.00', '$20.06', 'About $20.087', '$20.10'], answer: 2, explain: 'He fills 100 at $20.06 and 200 at $20.10: ($2,006 + $4,020) / 300 = about $20.087. His order walked the book.' },
    { q: 'Maya sells shares on Wednesday, November 25 (a normal trading day) and Thursday is Thanksgiving. Under T+1, when does the sale settle?', options: ['Wednesday', 'Thursday', 'Friday', 'The following Monday'], answer: 2, explain: 'T+1 means one business day later. Thanksgiving is a market holiday, so the next business day is Friday, which is an early-close day but still a business day.' },
    { q: 'Which pair of statements is BOTH correct?', options: ['SIPC covers market losses; FDIC covers brokerage accounts', 'The VIX tracks stock prices; a correction is a 20% drop', 'PFOF is illegal in the US; the NBBO is set by one exchange', 'The Dow is price-weighted; a 20% S&P 500 drop halts trading for the rest of the day'], answer: 3, explain: 'The Dow weights by share price, and Level 3 circuit breakers end trading for the day. The other pairs each contain a false statement.' },
    { q: 'Dev has $12,000 in a margin account and makes 5 day trades in 4 business days. Under the long-standing rule, what is most likely?', options: ['Nothing; the rule only applies to cash accounts', 'He gains extra buying power', 'He is flagged as a pattern day trader and restricted, since he is under $25,000 (check the current rule, which has been under revision)', 'His trades are canceled and refunded'], answer: 2, explain: 'Four or more day trades in five business days in a margin account triggers the PDT flag. Below the long-standing $25,000 minimum, day trading is restricted. FINRA has been revising the rule, so the current version may differ.' },
  ],
};
